# RAM++ semantic teacher: heavy optional runtime, never committed
ml/ram_plus/.venv/
ml/ram_plus/checkpoints/

# OCR audit: the OCR cache is derived from image bytes and is regenerable, and it
# can reach tens of MB. The reports themselves ARE committed; the cache is not.
# OCR audit derived artefacts.
#
# The readable answers (every .md report, provenance.json, search_index.sqlite)
# ARE committed. Two large binary products are not, because they are fully
# regenerable from the same command and would otherwise add ~64 MB to every
# clone:
#
#   evidence.jsonl    ~26 MB  one record per image
#   contact_sheets/   ~38 MB  130 rendered image grids
#
# They stay on disk after a run. `provenance.json` records the SHA-256 of
# evidence.jsonl, so a regenerated file can be verified byte-for-byte against a
# committed report's numbers:
#
#   python -m ml.gpo_ocr_audit
#   (Get-FileHash reports/ocr_audit/evidence.jsonl -Algorithm SHA256)
#
# Ontology review: contact sheets are rendered images, large and regenerable.
# The reports, concepts.json and the decision log ARE committed - the decision log
# especially, because it is the human audit trail.
reports/ontology_review/contact_sheets/
# The review workstation's server-side index and thumbnails. ~194 KB of JSON plus
# ~2.1 MB of JPEG, all rebuilt byte-identically by:
#
#   python -m ml.gpo_ontology_review build
#
# They are derived from committed inputs (evidence.jsonl + kb_export.json) and are
# gitignored for the same reason the contact sheets are: large, regenerable, and
# verified by the `index_sha256` recorded in the committed summary.json.
reports/ontology_review/workstation/
# Machine candidate labels. Regenerable in ~8s from the dataset and the wiki
# snapshot by `python -m ml.gpo_train.candidates`, and derived rather than authored,
# so the committed metadata (counts, per-class coverage, content hash) is what the
# tests and reports read.
reports/training_candidates/
# The human decision log. Never committed: a record here is a claim that a person
# made a judgement, and a fabricated one would be indistinguishable from a real one
# in the repository history. It lives on the reviewer's machine only.
reports/ontology_review/ontology_decisions.jsonl

reports/ocr_audit/.cache/
reports/ocr_audit/evidence.jsonl
reports/ocr_audit/contact_sheets/
reports/_smoke/
